/**
 * @mainpage Feature Isolation Project
 *
 * @section intro_sec Introduction
 *
 * The Feature Isolation Project analyzes Software Product Lines (SPLs) using the
 * set-theoretic model from the paper by Ulrich Eisenecker and Richard Müller,
 * *Software Product Lines and Features from the Perspective of Set Theory with
 * an Application to Feature Location*.
 *
 * The project parses SPL specifications, derives the valid system space from
 * Boolean and cardinality constraints, constructs a theory-native feature
 * registry over that system space, and computes feature-isolation witnesses.
 *
 * @section background_sec Theory overview
 *
 * The implementation is organized around four spaces:
 *
 * - the Boolean base feature set declared in the DSL,
 * - the candidate system space $P(F)$,
 * - the valid system space obtained by filtering candidates through the
 *   conjunction of asserted constraints,
 * - and the theory feature space derived from the valid systems.
 *
 * Features are classified from their simplified logical terms into the
 * categories `Impossible`, `Core`, `Fundamental`, `Not`, `And`, `Or`, and
 * `Complex`.
 *
 * @section components_sec Components
 *
 * - [Mathematical Helper Functions](ef fi_mathhelper)
 * - [Feature Analysis System](ef feature_analysis)
 * - [SPL Specification Parser](ef spl_parser)
 * - [Feature Isolation Analysis](ef feature_isolation)
 *
 * @section usage_sec Typical workflow
 *
 * 1. Parse an SPL specification:
 *    @code{.cpp}
 *    feature_isolation::spl_processor_t processor("example.spl");
 *    auto spec = processor.get_specification();
 *    @endcode
 *
 * 2. Derive the valid, available, and missing system spaces:
 *    @code{.cpp}
 *    auto valid = feature_isolation::valid_system_ids(spec);
 *    auto available = feature_isolation::available_valid_system_ids(spec);
 *    auto missing = feature_isolation::missing_valid_system_ids(spec);
 *    @endcode
 *
 * 3. Build the feature registry:
 *    @code{.cpp}
 *    feature_isolation::feature_analysis_t analysis(
 *        spec.m_F,
 *        feature_isolation::theory_system_space(spec),
 *        std::vector<std::string>(spec.m_feature_names.begin(),
 *                                 spec.m_feature_names.end()));
 *    @endcode
 *
 * 4. Run isolation analysis:
 *    @code{.cpp}
 *    feature_isolation::feature_isolation_t isolation("example.spl");
 *    isolation.print(std::cout);
 *    @endcode
 *
 * @section dsl_sec SPL DSL
 *
 * The accepted SPL language contains these top-level sections:
 *
 * - `SPL` — optional SPL name.
 * - `FEATURES` — mandatory Boolean base feature set.
 * - `CONSTRAINTS` — optional conjunction of `ASSERT` formulas.
 * - `AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS` — optional list of valid systems known to be
 *   available.
 * - `MISSING_SYSTEMS` — optional list of valid systems known to be missing.
 * - `CATEGORY_FILTER` — optional post-classification output filter over the
 *   theory-native categories.
 *
 * Example:
 * @code{.txt}
 * SPL MyProduct
 *
 * FEATURES 4 fx fy fz fw
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS
 *   ASSERT fx -> fy
 *   ASSERT !(fz && fw)
 *   ASSERT (fx || fz) -> fw
 *   ASSERT COUNT(fx, fy, fz) <= 2
 *   ASSERT COUNT(fy, fz, fw) IN [1,2]
 * END
 *
 * AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS 2 4 6 8 END
 * MISSING_SYSTEMS 10 12 END
 *
 * CATEGORY_FILTER Core Fundamental Not And Or Complex
 * @endcode
 *
 * Semantics:
 *
 * - If `CONSTRAINTS` is omitted, or if `CONSTRAINTS ... END` is empty, there
 *   are no constraints.
 * - `COUNT(...)` counts selected Boolean features and is part of the core
 *   theory-aligned DSL.
 * - `AVAILABLE_SYSTEMS` and `MISSING_SYSTEMS` may mention only valid systems.
 * - If both availability blocks are present, they must form a disjoint
 *   partition of the valid system space.
 * - If only one availability block is present, the other is derived by
 *   complement over the valid system space.
 * - If neither availability block is present, all valid systems are treated as
 *   available.
 * - `CATEGORY_FILTER` affects reporting only. It does not affect validity,
 *   feature generation, simplification, or classification.
 * - Attributes and repeated-feature multiplicity are outside the active DSL.
 *
 * @section isolation_sec Feature isolation
 *
 * For a theory feature $t$, the active isolation procedure partitions the
 * available valid systems into:
 *
 * - $I$: systems in which $t$ is present,
 * - $U$: systems in which $t$ is absent.
 *
 * It then computes:
 *
 * - the intersection of feature IDs over $I$,
 * - the union of feature IDs over $U$,
 * - and the flattened difference between those two sets.
 *
 * In the complete-information case the result may uniquely identify a feature.
 * With missing systems, the same witness may isolate a set of candidate
 * features instead. The output therefore distinguishes between `UNIQUE` and
 * `AMBIGUOUS` isolation results.
 *
 * @section references_sec References
 *
 * - Ulrich Eisenecker and Richard Müller,
 *   *Software Product Lines and Features from the Perspective of Set Theory
 *   with an Application to Feature Location*.
 *
 * @section notes_sec Additional notes
 *
 * - The active implementation evaluates its finite constraint language directly.
 * - The semantic source of truth is
 *   `external_documentation/theory_dsl_semantics.md`.
 * - CLI output aims to mirror the paper representation using `t_i`, `β(t_i)`,
 *   `ℓ_T(t_i)`, and paper-style system labels `s0`, `s1`, ...
 */
